WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.080
I wait in line for the bathroom every time

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.110
I'm in public. Like, every time.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.880
I'm a female, so I always wait

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.202
in line to use the bathroom.

00:00:08.202 --> 00:00:12.035
(all talking)

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:19.918
Ahhh.

00:00:19.918 --> 00:00:22.030
(crickets)

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:23.930
Narrator: For a typical busy bathroom,

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.770
women have to wait about
34 times as long as men.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.200
But, contrary to some stereotypes,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.130
it's not because they're busy chitchatting

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.410
or reapplying their makeup inside.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.573
So then why is there always a
line for the women's restroom?

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.550
Women spend as much as twice
as long in the bathroom as men,

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.378
and that's because they menstruate,

00:00:44.378 --> 00:00:46.478
they have more layers
of clothes to remove,

00:00:46.478 --> 00:00:48.911
they always have to enter a stall,

00:00:48.911 --> 00:00:50.820
and they're far more likely to have kids

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.790
or elderly people with them.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.860
But these are just contributing factors

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.930
to a much larger problem.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.218
Kathryn Anthony: Well, in
most of the building stock,

00:00:58.218 --> 00:01:02.190
there are fewer fixtures for
women than there are for men.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.230
Narrator: That's Kathryn
Anthony, a professor

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.400
of architecture at the
University of Illinois

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.550
at Urbana-Champaign.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.130
Anthony: It is definitely a
subtle, but very powerful,

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.670
form of gender discrimination.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.210
No question about that,
it is plain and simple.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.740
There's no disguising it.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.610
Narrator: She says that in many buildings,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.930
the men's and women's
rooms are the same size,

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.360
but because urinals take up less space,

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.330
men's rooms have more toilets,

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:28.340
which contributes to less
waiting time for men.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.520
To figure out just how much less,

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.780
queuing theorists at Ghent
University developed a model.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.920
First, they considered bathrooms
that were the same size

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.170
where men have 12 toilets
and women have just 10.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.810
Then, they accounted for the
time spent per bathroom visit

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.110
for women and men and
the speed of traffic.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.870
The model showed that if someone
arrives every five seconds,

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.950
on average, women will
wait more than six minutes

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.350
while men will wait just 11 seconds.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.110
So then what would a
better toilet ratio be?

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.726
Anthony: I would say the ratio should be

00:01:59.726 --> 00:02:01.426
well more than 1 to 1.

00:02:01.426 --> 00:02:02.580
Narrator: She's right.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.580
According to this model,
the women's room should have

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:07.670
at least 50% more toilets
than the men's room,

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.290
as they show here.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:13.280
Only then would men and women
have nearly equal wait times.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.570
Now, the US is getting
closer to this target

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.210
thanks to the so-called
"potty parity" movement.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:23.145
It took off in 1987 when
a California state senator

00:02:23.145 --> 00:02:26.160
went to a Tchaikovsky concert
at the Hollywood Bowl.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.650
There, he waited for
more than half an hour

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.610
for his wife to use the bathroom.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.010
Anthony: He realized just
how long it took for her,

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.320
and that was unfair.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.929
She complained, and he
complained and said,

00:02:35.929 --> 00:02:37.580
"wait a minute, this isn't right."

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.460
Narrator: Soon after, he introduced a bill

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.898
to the California Senate, which demanded

00:02:41.898 --> 00:02:44.763
more toilets for women in
large, new public buildings.

00:02:44.763 --> 00:02:47.582
Anthony: That started a
wave of legislation across

00:02:47.582 --> 00:02:50.850
some states and also
cities and municipalities.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.620
Narrator: At least 21 states
and several cities have

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.690
since passed potty-parity policies.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.500
In 2005, for example, New
York City passed legislation

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.010
that requires a 2-to-1
toilet ratio in places

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.030
of public assembly, such
as baseball stadiums.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.670
But here's the thing: Even
where these laws exist,

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.330
you still find long lines
for the women's room,

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.242
and that's because they apply only

00:03:15.242 --> 00:03:19.720
to buildings that are new or
undergoing major renovations,

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.960
which leaves thousands of older
buildings, such as theaters,

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:24.813
with too few toilets for women.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.770
Sure, it's a hassle to wait,

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:29.890
but that's not the only problem.

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:32.640
Long term, it can actually
affect your health.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.880
Anthony: A lot of women can end up

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.920
with urinary tract infections

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.960
and other kinds of health issues.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:39.400
Narrator: So, what can be done?

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.720
Aside from waiting for
renovations or new construction,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.890
there's a new trend:
gender-neutral bathrooms.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:47.600
Anthony: That's a way
to alleviate the problem

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.602
of potty parity altogether,

00:03:49.602 --> 00:03:53.125
so either nobody waits or everybody waits,

00:03:53.125 --> 00:03:55.970
but you don't have one group
waiting more than another.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:57.070
Narrator: And let's be real.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.170
Women are already using the men's room.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.610
If the line in the
women's room is too long,

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.390
I will never hesitate
to go to the men's room.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:06.590
I probably go to the men's
room a couple times a year

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:08.700
when it's a dire situation.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.570
I will literally go in the
men's bathroom probably more

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:13.460
than I will go in the
women's bathroom in New York.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.520
I also use the men's room
a lot when I'm waiting.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:17.530
Narrator: Because, well,

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:21.113
we all know the
alternative is a lot worse.

